# fnvalues 4.4: a callee given one known body's code is compiled again with its calls through it direct - the prelude's
# Sort and its whole chain for a lambda, and msort, recursive, for a named function: the recursion calls the clone
import "std/io"

fn before(x I64, y I64) Bool { return x < y }

# a recursive merge sort of a[lo:hi], through less
fn msort(a mut Array<I64>&, tmp mut Array<I64>&, lo I64, hi I64, less fn&(x I64, y I64) Bool) {
    if hi - lo < 2: return
    m := (lo + hi) / 2
    msort(a, tmp, lo, m, less)
    msort(a, tmp, m, hi, less)
    i, j, k := lo, m, lo
    for i < m and j < hi {
        if less(a[j], a[i]) {
            tmp[k] = a[j]
            j++
        } else {
            tmp[k] = a[i]
            i++
        }
        k++
    }
    for i < m {
        tmp[k] = a[i]
        i++
        k++
    }
    for j < hi {
        tmp[k] = a[j]
        j++
        k++
    }
    for x in range lo, hi { a[x] = tmp[x] }
}

fn main() ? {
    n I64 = 100000
    a := Array<I64>(n)
    b := Array<I64>(n)
    s I64 = 1
    for i in range n {
        s = s * 6364136223846793005 + 1442695040888963407
        a[i] = s >> 33
        b[i] = a[i]
    }
    a.Sort(fn(x I64, y I64) Bool: return x < y)
    tmp := Array<I64>(n)
    msort(b, tmp, 0, n, before)
    for i in range 1, n {
        if a[i - 1] > a[i] or a[i] != b[i]: unreachable "unsorted"
    }
    try io.PutLine($a[0] " " $a[n - 1])
}
